WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.417
(soft music)

00:00:05.749 --> 00:00:08.309
- Well, welcome back to another
episode of the "Addy Hour."

00:00:08.309 --> 00:00:10.380
It's my privilege, of course,

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.050
to be able to continue
hosting these conversations.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.810
And, today, I'm absolutely
honored to be able to host

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:18.349
former Congressman Patrick J. Kennedy

00:00:18.349 --> 00:00:21.390
for a conversation about mental health,

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.309
about politics and about advocacy.

00:00:23.309 --> 00:00:25.080
So, obviously, this is a name

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.330
that I'm sure is familiar
to all of you as listeners,

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:29.190
but I want to do due diligence,

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:30.709
and do a little bit of an introduction

00:00:30.709 --> 00:00:33.150
for Congressman Kennedy as well.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:34.860
So during his time in Congress,

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:38.130
Patrick J. Kennedy was the
lead author of the landmark

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:41.070
Mental Health Parity and
Addiction Equity Act,

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:43.079
also known as the Federal Parity Law,

00:00:43.079 --> 00:00:45.810
which requires insurers to cover treatment

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.850
for mental health, and
substance use disorders,

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:49.470
with no more restrictively

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:53.070
than they would for treatment
for illnesses of the body.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:54.480
As founder of the Kennedy Forum,

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.300
he now unites advocates, policymakers,

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.720
and business leaders to advance
evidence-based practices,

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.067
and policies in mental
health and addiction.

00:01:04.067 --> 00:01:06.990
In 2017, he was appointed to
the President's Commission

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:10.350
on Combating Drug Addiction
and the Opioid Crisis.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:11.250
He currently serves

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:13.605
as co-chair of the
National Action Alliance,

00:01:13.605 --> 00:01:16.710
specifically for the Suicide
Prevention's Mental Health,

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:20.460
and Suicide Prevention
National Response to COVID-19.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.955
And he serves as co-chair of
the Bipartisan Policy Center's

00:01:23.955 --> 00:01:27.420
Behavioral Health Integration Task Force.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.610
So someone who has been about this work

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.440
for a very long time.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.810
I did mention to the congressman
before we got on the air

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:35.936
that I had the privilege of
actually hearing him speak

00:01:35.936 --> 00:01:39.450
at an event back in
2014 here in New Haven,

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:41.040
a Global Health and the Arts event.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:44.220
And I was struck by just his
candor in sharing his story,

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.680
and really trying to move things forward.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.660
I will also say that on a personal note,

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:52.110
it's been just a joy and
an honor for me to see

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:54.150
the ways that he's continued
to be about this work

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:55.560
on so many different levels,

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.810
really addressing things like stigma,

00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:59.335
of course, thinking about policy,

00:01:59.335 --> 00:02:02.400
also thinking about
access to care, insurance,

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.530
but then also tying in,
and making sure that

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:07.470
we have an understanding of
what happens in mental illness,

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:08.880
and understanding the brain,

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.400
and really having the
research to move that forward.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:12.690
So I'm absolutely honored, again,

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:15.420
to be able to welcome former
Congressman Patrick J. Kennedy

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.070
to the "Addy Hour."

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.860
- Thank you, Dr. Addy, it's
a pleasure to be with you.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:23.400
And I just wanna thank you
on behalf of all my fellows

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:27.840
who have benefited from
professions of psychiatry,

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:29.580
and psychology for all you've done

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:34.470
to try to face the challenge
that we're facing as a nation

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.480
by dedicating your careers,

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.620
and your mission to really helping people

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:42.090
not only on a medical level,

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:47.090
but just really help them
build back some agency,

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.620
and reclaim their lives,

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:52.110
and no longer kind of be marginalized

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:55.890
because society would do
that to them ordinarily.

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:57.630
And so thank you so much.

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:00.736
You do have a spiritual
dimension to your job,

00:03:00.736 --> 00:03:03.810
which is underappreciated

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:08.810
because when you're restored
to physical wellbeing,

00:03:08.816 --> 00:03:11.400
the transformations to your family,

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.500
and community are so enormous,

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:17.460
but that's not often appreciated
in the work that you do.

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:18.510
So thank you for that.

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:22.230
- Of course, and thank
you for those kind words.

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:24.480
And in a lot of ways this podcast,

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:27.420
in addition to what I do
on a professional level

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:28.800
has been a joy

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:30.780
to be able to pull so many
different people together

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.670
as you've alluded to as well,

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:34.560
even as you talked about
the spiritual component,

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:36.967
and just to be able to have
different people come together

00:03:36.967 --> 00:03:39.030
in this podcast forum,

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:41.850
and even in the event
that you did back in 2014,

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:44.940
to see researchers and
clinicians and actors,

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:47.220
and public advocates
all in the same space,

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.200
trying to move these things forward.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:50.760
So I know we're complimenting
each other here,

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:52.800
but I think it's just
important to acknowledge

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:53.880
how important that is

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:56.330
in terms of really trying
to move things forward.

00:03:57.807 --> 00:04:00.540
It's just an honor to do that.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.068
- Gratitude is a mindset,

00:04:02.068 --> 00:04:05.970
and an attitude I've learned
to cultivate in recovery

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:07.715
from addiction and mental illness.

00:04:07.715 --> 00:04:10.314
The default mode for my brain

00:04:10.314 --> 00:04:12.870
is to think about the negative,

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:17.870
to ruminate about my fears
of losing what I have,

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:20.130
or not getting what I want.

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:23.310
And part of my mental wellbeing

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:26.520
is to transform that thought process,

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:29.040
which is negative to one where,

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:32.550
because I'm trained through
cognitive behavioral therapy,

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:35.250
which is what 12-step recovery is to me

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:38.280
to always look every day in a humble way,

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:41.940
how I can be of service to someone else

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:45.690
that gets me outta my own
self-centered thinking.

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:48.990
And so I wanna say that
this is not outta some,

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:52.920
like, pure adulation,
or sucking up to you.

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:56.220
I just wanna really point
out to your listeners

00:04:56.220 --> 00:05:00.160
that this is about
living life differently.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:05.160
It saves us trying to
be open to other people.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.717
And so I was grateful to
be invited to be on this.

00:05:09.717 --> 00:05:11.250
- Oh, of course, of course.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:12.210
And that's so well said,

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:14.250
and I appreciate even the
way that you've already

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:15.900
just start to take us on your journey,

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:18.210
and to show us the importance of that

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:20.040
as we walk through
those different aspects,

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:22.530
and have that different approach
and that different mindset,

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:23.640
all the things that we can do

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:24.960
with cognitive behavioral therapy,

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.137
and how important that is

00:05:26.137 --> 00:05:28.875
for us as we're walking
through these journeys, so.

00:05:28.875 --> 00:05:30.630
I'm already appreciative.

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:31.463
I knew you'd be able to

00:05:31.463 --> 00:05:33.960
bring that level of candor and insight,

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.300
and grateful that you've already
even started to share that

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:37.233
in this moment.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:40.170
And even to follow-up on
that as my listeners know,

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:42.180
I always like to start out,
and you've done this already,

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:43.140
just checking in,

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:45.870
and seeing how people are doing
in the midst of everything

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:48.330
that we're continuing to
experience as a society.

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:50.040
Obviously, there are so many
things that are going on

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:51.810
at this point in time,

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:53.743
even as we we're in the
middle of the hearings

00:05:53.743 --> 00:05:56.520
around the January 6th situation,

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:58.500
all the things that we're
continuing to walk through

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:01.110
just as we continue to
navigate the pandemic,

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:02.490
as people are still getting sick,

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:04.650
as we're experiencing

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:06.750
tragic situation after tragic situation,

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:08.630
but then as we also have reasons for hope

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:11.130
in the work that we do
as you've alluded to,

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:12.690
and just the joy that we can have

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:14.940
from being in community
with one another, so.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:17.250
That was a long-winded
introduction, but just to say,

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:18.083
I wanted to start there,

00:06:18.083 --> 00:06:21.300
and see just how you're
doing at this point in time.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:22.323
- I appreciate that.

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:28.160
I really feel the biology in my brain

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:31.530
is where I sink or swim,

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:35.659
because I really feel overwhelmed

00:06:35.659 --> 00:06:38.670
throughout the week here and there,

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:41.580
but because of that issue of
cognitive behavioral therapy,

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:45.161
'cause I know that feelings aren't facts,

00:06:45.161 --> 00:06:47.360
but in recovery I know that

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:50.840
I used to drink and drug over feelings,

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:53.400
even though they're not facts,

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:55.620
like, so I learn in recovery

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:59.250
to take a gratitude list of
all the great things in my life

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:03.180
because I have to do a reality check

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:05.160
because even if with everything,

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:09.570
as you said is so unhinged
around us in the world today,

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:12.141
and we're really off-kilter in my view,

00:07:12.141 --> 00:07:15.720
in our culture, society, just in large,

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:18.840
the toxicity of our political culture,

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:22.530
the obviously existential
crisis of our environment,

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:27.530
the shootings, the feeling
of safety all jeopardized,

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:32.910
very big issues that
could really sink anyone.

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:36.690
The key here is how do
I respond and react?

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:40.590
That's how I'm taught in
recovery is now what's going on?

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:42.878
How do I react to what's going on?

00:07:42.878 --> 00:07:46.230
And I do it by then talking about,

00:07:46.230 --> 00:07:48.123
like you said, the hopeful things.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:52.440
My four-year-old came
into my bed this morning,

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:55.170
and put his hand on my face,

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:59.905
like, that his affect
that he's so affectionate.

00:07:59.905 --> 00:08:03.197
I lay there and was just soak that in

00:08:03.197 --> 00:08:06.390
'cause it was like one of
the best parts of the day.

00:08:06.390 --> 00:08:11.390
Yesterday I got to give him a mop-up bin

00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:14.760
where you put all the water to do the mop.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:19.760
I fit him in, he fit
into this bin of water,

00:08:19.923 --> 00:08:22.500
and had a bubble bath.

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:24.630
And it was just I just loved.

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:27.654
Myself, like, I can see
that as a joy today,

00:08:27.654 --> 00:08:32.010
and that relieves the
tension and the stress

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:35.600
of having to kind of ruminate
about all that's going on

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:38.494
in the world like every other American,

00:08:38.494 --> 00:08:42.723
anyone else in the world I could
easily get carried away by.

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:47.100
- Wow, such a helpful perspective,
and great stories, too.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:49.738
And as someone who is
also a neuroscientist,

00:08:49.738 --> 00:08:52.380
I'm resonating with what
you're saying about the brain,

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:54.306
and thinking about all
those aspects as well.

00:08:54.306 --> 00:08:56.310
And even topics that we've talked about,

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:58.530
even just our interactions
with our family members,

00:08:58.530 --> 00:09:00.000
and what that does to our brains,

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:01.530
and the oxytocin that gets released.

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:03.476
And as you said, that just
helps us kind of move through,

00:09:03.476 --> 00:09:08.070
and even as you're so proactive
about that mindset shift,

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:09.497
and really using that to move
through, so I appreciate that.

00:09:09.497 --> 00:09:11.400
- Well, you know, Dr. Addy,

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:15.490
I love the neuroscience because I read

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:20.970
the "Dopamine Nation" by one
of your colleagues in Stanford

00:09:20.970 --> 00:09:24.450
talk about how we're
constantly in our society

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:25.980
trying to push all the buttons

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:28.440
to flood our brains with dopamine.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:31.230
And another way of saying
is we're engaged in kind of

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:36.230
addictive behaviors to try to
feed our feeling of anxiety,

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:37.620
to medicate that.

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:40.200
And you can do it beyond
just drugs and alcohol.

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:41.220
I mean, I'm in recovery

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:43.020
from active use of drugs and alcohol,

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:46.110
but I can feel those
dopamine receptors by, quote,

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:49.319
acting out in any number
of the seven deadly sins

00:09:49.319 --> 00:09:51.720
'cause they give me a short burst,

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:54.300
but in that neuroscience story,

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:56.940
it shows me that I have a hangover,

00:09:56.940 --> 00:10:00.720
whether I have drank too
much, or drugged too much,

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:05.720
or act out too much on any
number of the dopamine stimuli

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:09.480
that I could try to use to self-medicate.

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:11.580
Anyway, what I'm saying to your audience

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:14.820
is that understanding the neuroscience

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:16.890
really helps me think about this

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:19.890
more than just as a philosophical issue.

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:22.560
It explains why I wanna be in recovery

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:27.128
because that actually
makes me feel better.

00:10:27.128 --> 00:10:30.690
I love you're mentioning the oxytocin

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:34.890
because I hold onto my babies,
and I get all close up.

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:38.730
I got five kids because
I know that it's like,

00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:42.870
it's not medicating for
me just to give them hugs.

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:44.760
And I know for them it's key

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:47.310
because I know their
brains are developing,

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:50.190
and I want them to be solid foundations.

00:10:50.190 --> 00:10:53.070
And it's not enough for me
just to not drink and drug

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:55.950
because I know that
makes them feel better,

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:59.187
but because I wanna reinforce
kind of positive feelings

00:10:59.187 --> 00:11:03.450
of agency for them and awareness, so.

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:04.283
- That's great.

00:11:04.283 --> 00:11:06.150
I love hearing you share about
that and just all the things

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:07.860
that that does to our reward centers

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:09.090
as we talk about as well.

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:10.440
And even as you were talking,

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:13.020
as I do addiction
research and we find that,

00:11:13.020 --> 00:11:14.880
even if we're doing
things in rats or mice,

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:16.500
if they play and have that time,

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:19.590
it makes it much less likely
they'll take more substance,

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:20.423
or take to excess,

00:11:20.423 --> 00:11:23.760
so it's wonderful to just
hear you elaborate on that,

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:25.680
and give these stories as well.

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:27.330
And just to hear the
passion in your voice,

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:30.540
because I can really see how
that passion has infiltrated

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:32.280
into all of the work that you've done

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:33.690
on so many different fronts,

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:35.670
especially with the Kennedy Forum,

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:37.269
and so many other things.

00:11:37.269 --> 00:11:39.450
So as you've been
highlighting those pieces,

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:41.820
I wonder if you'd also just
share a little bit about

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:43.890
how the Kennedy Forum came about.

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:46.590
What was the journey that
brought you to this place?

00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:48.690
And then at some point we'll
delve into some of the work

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:52.320
that you do within the
organization as well.

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:56.610
- Well, I got elected as
a state legislator at 21

00:11:56.610 --> 00:11:58.140
in Rhode Island I was the youngest member.

00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:01.020
I was elected at 26 to Congress

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:02.460
as the youngest member of Congress,

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:05.970
and then chosen to be in leadership at 31.

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:08.490
I can guarantee you, Dr.
Addy, none of it had to do

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:11.250
with my last name being
Kennedy whatsoever.

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:14.130
It was all my good looks and
personality that got me so far.

00:12:14.130 --> 00:12:15.280
- Of course, of course.

00:12:16.470 --> 00:12:19.860
- So there's something very
powerful about my uncle,

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:23.130
President Kennedy's new frontier,

00:12:23.130 --> 00:12:26.310
the message of hopefulness that he gave

00:12:26.310 --> 00:12:29.040
when he embarked on the Peace Corps.

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:31.252
When he set a vision for going to the moon

00:12:31.252 --> 00:12:33.270
in under 10 years,

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:36.240
which was thought of as being
way over his skis at the time.

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:41.240
And it's just aspirational
aspects of his leadership.

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:43.590
And that still resonates to people today

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:47.310
who are looking for narrative and context.

00:12:47.310 --> 00:12:51.630
So I've woven his work on
civil rights, for example,

00:12:51.630 --> 00:12:53.850
as the first president to speak about that

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:56.370
as a moral issue when he said,

00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:59.070
who amongst us would trade
the color of their skin,

00:12:59.070 --> 00:13:01.050
and then be content with those

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:02.640
who counsel patience and delay?

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:04.830
In other words, the Golden Rule.

00:13:04.830 --> 00:13:06.360
How would you like to be treated?

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:09.660
Think about what it's like to
have different colored skin,

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:13.465
and be living in this country
where you're still feeling

00:13:13.465 --> 00:13:16.950
as though the country doesn't accept you.

00:13:16.950 --> 00:13:20.970
And the racialization of many
institutions in this country

00:13:20.970 --> 00:13:25.970
that still is so demoralizing
to our friends and neighbors

00:13:25.993 --> 00:13:28.590
who have different colored skin

00:13:28.590 --> 00:13:31.740
who don't feel fully accepted in America.

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:32.730
Anyway, my point is,

00:13:32.730 --> 00:13:36.433
is that these notions are very helpful

00:13:36.433 --> 00:13:39.090
when I'm advocating for mental health.

00:13:39.090 --> 00:13:44.090
And, ironically, my family
was very stigmatizing

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:45.840
on mental illness.

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:48.660
They were real visionaries and leaders,

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:50.730
but President Kennedy signed

00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:52.630
the first Community Mental Health Act.

00:13:54.832 --> 00:13:58.350
He grew up in a family
where no one spoke about

00:13:58.350 --> 00:14:00.780
his sister's intellectual disability.

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:03.360
In fact, after my grandfather

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:06.090
chose to have a lobotomy for her,

00:14:06.090 --> 00:14:08.970
and that was clearly unsuccessful,

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:13.441
and she was kind of put out
in a monastery in Wisconsin,

00:14:13.441 --> 00:14:15.930
never to be seen or heard of again.

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:16.763
- Wow.

00:14:16.763 --> 00:14:17.596
- Right?

00:14:17.596 --> 00:14:21.333
That shame was so palpable
in my father's life.

00:14:21.333 --> 00:14:24.725
And then, thankfully, my Aunt Eunice,

00:14:24.725 --> 00:14:27.450
finally got the approval from her brother

00:14:27.450 --> 00:14:31.530
to speak about it openly after
he was elected president.

00:14:31.530 --> 00:14:35.040
And then from that came
the Special Olympics,

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:37.770
which is now in over 190
countries around the world,

00:14:37.770 --> 00:14:39.870
and transforming the view

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:42.060
towards people with
intellectual disabilities,

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:45.720
but my point is that the silence and shame

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:50.010
that accompanied that terrible situation

00:14:50.010 --> 00:14:51.300
with my Aunt Rosemary,

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:54.480
which by the way, was also
fueled by a psychiatric illness.

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:57.420
Many believed that she had
a kind of form of bipolar,

00:14:57.420 --> 00:14:59.520
which coupled with her
intellectual disability

00:14:59.520 --> 00:15:01.350
led her to outbursts

00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:03.780
that made my grandparents
very uncomfortable,

00:15:03.780 --> 00:15:05.793
which is what led to the lobotomy.

00:15:06.810 --> 00:15:09.540
It's about talking about this in a way

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:11.890
that isn't stigmatizing.

00:15:11.890 --> 00:15:16.740
And when I did mental
health and addiction,

00:15:16.740 --> 00:15:21.330
that was very shameful in my family.

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:25.140
My mother suffered from profound
alcoholism and depression,

00:15:25.140 --> 00:15:26.910
my father self-medicated.

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:28.050
He was traumatized

00:15:28.050 --> 00:15:33.050
by his brother's assassinations
with murders, I mean,

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:36.739
but it was still that way,

00:15:36.739 --> 00:15:39.810
and my father is a very
forward thinking guy,

00:15:39.810 --> 00:15:41.910
but it was hard for him to get past

00:15:41.910 --> 00:15:45.060
his generation's view of
mental health and addiction

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:49.380
as a character flaw as
opposed to a chemistry issue.

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:54.270
And I found that in the
course of my advocating,

00:15:54.270 --> 00:15:59.270
I was trying to break the shackles

00:15:59.580 --> 00:16:04.580
of living in that silence
and the imprisonment,

00:16:05.130 --> 00:16:07.890
because not only did I
suffer from these illnesses,

00:16:07.890 --> 00:16:10.560
as I said, my whole family did,

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:14.310
and there was something
I knew I had to do.

00:16:14.310 --> 00:16:16.593
And by the way, I didn't
choose to do this.

00:16:17.820 --> 00:16:20.130
The guy that I was in drug rehab with

00:16:20.130 --> 00:16:23.790
sold his story of being in drug rehab

00:16:23.790 --> 00:16:27.720
to the "National Inquirer" which outed me,

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:30.638
and, Dr. Addy, that ended
up being the best thing

00:16:30.638 --> 00:16:33.660
that ever happened to me in
my life because instead of,

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:37.590
like, making this a
closely guarded secret,

00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:41.550
when I got to Washington
I had already been outed.

00:16:41.550 --> 00:16:45.060
So I could put my name
first on a bill that said

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:46.740
mental health ought to be treated the same

00:16:46.740 --> 00:16:48.960
because unlike my colleagues

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:50.670
who are much more senior than me,

00:16:50.670 --> 00:16:53.490
who could have been the
sponsors of that bill,

00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:56.160
none of them wanted to answer
why they were a sponsor

00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:57.570
of the bill that had both the words,

00:16:57.570 --> 00:17:00.390
mental and addiction in the title,

00:17:00.390 --> 00:17:02.370
'cause, of course, press
would naturally ask,

00:17:02.370 --> 00:17:04.738
well, have you had any of
these issues in your life?

00:17:04.738 --> 00:17:06.600
No one else wanted to answer them,

00:17:06.600 --> 00:17:09.960
but because I was a Kennedy,
I was exposed early.

00:17:09.960 --> 00:17:12.570
I hated it at the time,

00:17:12.570 --> 00:17:14.580
but it ended up being
one of the best things

00:17:14.580 --> 00:17:16.110
that ever happened to me.

00:17:16.110 --> 00:17:19.860
- Wow, that's amazing to hear
the backstory for that too,

00:17:19.860 --> 00:17:20.841
'cause even as you were talking,

00:17:20.841 --> 00:17:23.250
'cause as you talked about the stigma

00:17:23.250 --> 00:17:24.240
kind of being pervasive,

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:26.580
what it was that really
helped you push through that,

00:17:26.580 --> 00:17:28.080
to say that there needs to be a shift,

00:17:28.080 --> 00:17:30.150
and it sounds like there was some,

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:31.860
I don't know if serendipity
is the right word,

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:34.440
but not that you wouldn't have wanted to,

00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:35.370
but that there was a situation

00:17:35.370 --> 00:17:37.260
that allowed you to step into that.

00:17:37.260 --> 00:17:39.780
And so I really applaud
you for making that shift.

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:41.940
And as you alluded to it's not that others

00:17:41.940 --> 00:17:43.680
wouldn't have been
navigating that as well.

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:45.330
I mean, if we think about
our current statistics,

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:48.390
one in five who are living
currently with a mental illness.

00:17:48.390 --> 00:17:51.000
So when we think about people
in government and politics,

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:52.072
that includes all of us.

00:17:52.072 --> 00:17:54.540
So for you to really be
able to take that step.

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:56.510
and move it forward, I
think is so important.

00:17:56.510 --> 00:17:59.902
Would you say you've seen
shifts in the perspective

00:17:59.902 --> 00:18:02.280
amongst when you are in Congress,

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:04.290
amongst your colleagues
for moving that forward?

00:18:04.290 --> 00:18:07.200
Because reading through
kind of the process of it,

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:09.840
and I'm sure that there was pushback

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:11.190
with that along the way as well.

00:18:11.190 --> 00:18:13.050
So what was that process like,

00:18:13.050 --> 00:18:14.880
and what have you seen shift over time?

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:16.980
- So because these are chronic illnesses,

00:18:16.980 --> 00:18:19.680
just because I went to treatment at 17

00:18:19.680 --> 00:18:21.420
didn't mean I was done.

00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:23.280
I constantly grappled with this,

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:25.408
and, of course, I still
tried to keep it secret

00:18:25.408 --> 00:18:30.270
because, of course, I assumed
even as the champion of parity

00:18:30.270 --> 00:18:31.127
that I was supposed to be better, right?

00:18:31.127 --> 00:18:32.730
I waws supposed to be okay.

00:18:32.730 --> 00:18:34.320
And, of course, our health care system

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:36.930
never treated my illness
the way it does my asthma,

00:18:36.930 --> 00:18:39.720
which is a chronic illness
and needs constant attention.

00:18:39.720 --> 00:18:44.720
It just is a kind of acute
episodic treatment system, right?

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:47.370
That doesn't reimburse for
the chronic care nature

00:18:47.370 --> 00:18:48.720
of these illnesses.

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:52.710
So that's a long way of
saying when I got a DWI,

00:18:52.710 --> 00:18:57.710
because my illness still
hadn't been succeeded

00:18:58.290 --> 00:19:00.509
in terms of its treatment

00:19:00.509 --> 00:19:03.603
because these are chronic illnesses.

00:19:06.096 --> 00:19:08.910
It was very shameful
when I went to treatment,

00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:11.790
and it looked as though I was
gonna have to leave Congress.

00:19:11.790 --> 00:19:16.790
I got a lot of letters
from colleagues telling me

00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:18.270
that they were pulling for me,

00:19:18.270 --> 00:19:21.750
which really folks that I
had never talked to before

00:19:21.750 --> 00:19:23.610
when I was serving with them.

00:19:23.610 --> 00:19:26.309
When I got back from treatment,

00:19:26.309 --> 00:19:29.203
I went and visited many
of them to thank them.

00:19:29.203 --> 00:19:33.420
And it was the first time I'd
ever had them tell me about

00:19:33.420 --> 00:19:35.370
their own challenges.

00:19:35.370 --> 00:19:38.220
And then I realized I
was in a unique place

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:41.370
because I was the only one
who had mental illness,

00:19:41.370 --> 00:19:43.980
and addiction tattooed to his forehead

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:45.990
because I was the only one

00:19:45.990 --> 00:19:48.600
of their colleagues that they knew,

00:19:48.600 --> 00:19:52.350
when, in fact, now I had
the opposite benefit.

00:19:52.350 --> 00:19:56.160
I had the eyes on the fact
that so many of my colleagues

00:19:56.160 --> 00:19:59.190
had similar challenges in their own lives,

00:19:59.190 --> 00:20:00.280
and in their family's lives

00:20:00.280 --> 00:20:02.850
that none of the other
ones of them knew about,

00:20:02.850 --> 00:20:07.050
but I had that secret perspective,

00:20:07.050 --> 00:20:09.300
which I kept secret
'cause they asked me to,

00:20:09.300 --> 00:20:12.693
but I met a lot of my colleagues.

00:20:14.520 --> 00:20:19.020
Some of them who told me
very harrowing stories

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:21.720
about their own family's
mental illness and addiction

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:23.070
voted against

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:26.250
our Mental Health Parity
and Addiction Equity Act.

00:20:26.250 --> 00:20:29.040
And I never outed them
or challenged them on it.

00:20:29.040 --> 00:20:30.810
I did talk to them later on.

00:20:30.810 --> 00:20:33.090
I said, how could you have
voted a no on that bill?

00:20:33.090 --> 00:20:37.380
I thought you told me about
suicide in your family,

00:20:37.380 --> 00:20:39.847
your daughter's eating
disorder, and so forth.

00:20:39.847 --> 00:20:43.110
And they said, "Well,
Patrick, I don't come from

00:20:43.110 --> 00:20:45.750
the same part of the
country you come from.

00:20:45.750 --> 00:20:48.183
I come from the buckle of the Bible Belt.

00:20:49.080 --> 00:20:51.870
Your constituents may be
all right reelecting you,

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:55.740
but I can't be sure mine would
reelect me if they knew."

00:20:55.740 --> 00:20:58.470
And then they said something
even more powerful.

00:20:58.470 --> 00:21:00.840
They said, "Frankly,
when I think about it,

00:21:00.840 --> 00:21:04.990
if my child ever felt

00:21:06.030 --> 00:21:10.740
that they were exposed, I can't
be sure what they would do."

00:21:10.740 --> 00:21:12.480
Which, of course, the
end of that sentence is

00:21:12.480 --> 00:21:14.794
I can't be sure whether they
wouldn't take their lives

00:21:14.794 --> 00:21:19.080
because of the shame of
these illnesses, so, I mean,

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:23.820
that still shows you
that we made progress,

00:21:23.820 --> 00:21:27.423
but there's still a lot of
caveats to that progress.

00:21:28.320 --> 00:21:30.990
I also tell people,
like, we passed this law,

00:21:30.990 --> 00:21:33.450
but it wasn't because everybody
woke up one day and said,

00:21:33.450 --> 00:21:35.645
oh, wouldn't it be a good
thing that we cover the brain

00:21:35.645 --> 00:21:38.760
like we would every
other organ of the body?

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:41.580
We passed this law because Chris Dodd

00:21:41.580 --> 00:21:43.415
was a friend of my family's,

00:21:43.415 --> 00:21:47.670
and our bill in the House was
the better of the two bills.

00:21:47.670 --> 00:21:52.080
And I got Chris to pass
our Bill H.R. 1424,

00:21:52.080 --> 00:21:56.580
by writing the whole Economic
Stabilization Act of 2008,

00:21:56.580 --> 00:21:58.800
which is the Toxic Asset Relief Program.

00:21:58.800 --> 00:22:02.323
In other words, the seven
to $800 billion bailout

00:22:02.323 --> 00:22:05.010
of Jamie Dimon, and all the big bankers

00:22:05.010 --> 00:22:07.110
that got us into this
mess in the first place

00:22:07.110 --> 00:22:10.637
with our meltdown and
banking crisis in 2008.

00:22:10.637 --> 00:22:13.470
That's how we got the Parity Bill passed.

00:22:13.470 --> 00:22:17.730
So when George Bush signed the Parity Law,

00:22:17.730 --> 00:22:19.200
he was really signing it

00:22:19.200 --> 00:22:22.650
because it contained the
provisions to get our country

00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:25.410
out of getting into another
financial great depression.

00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:26.453
He wasn't signing it

00:22:26.453 --> 00:22:30.873
because it mandated
coverage for depression, so.

00:22:32.970 --> 00:22:36.210
The bottom line is up until COVID,

00:22:36.210 --> 00:22:39.000
I haven't seen any
traction on mental health.

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:42.090
And since the time I
left Congress 'til today,

00:22:42.090 --> 00:22:44.400
I have been pushing for the enforcement

00:22:44.400 --> 00:22:47.820
of the Mental Health Parity
Law really to no avail.

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:50.049
All of the leading psychiatric hospitals,

00:22:50.049 --> 00:22:53.228
and addiction treatment providers

00:22:53.228 --> 00:22:55.770
really did not put their
shoulder behind this,

00:22:55.770 --> 00:22:57.630
even though this was good for them.

00:22:57.630 --> 00:23:00.420
And the reason was is because
they didn't wanna offend

00:23:00.420 --> 00:23:03.630
the big payers because all
of them were so dependent

00:23:03.630 --> 00:23:07.367
on the Uniteds, and Aetnas,
and Anthems, and Cignas.

00:23:08.430 --> 00:23:10.440
And so it was interesting to me,

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:14.130
the very people who should
have been the most supportive

00:23:14.130 --> 00:23:15.780
were kind of the most reticent.

00:23:15.780 --> 00:23:16.680
And I thought to myself,

00:23:16.680 --> 00:23:20.400
if we can't get the very providers
who benefit from this law

00:23:20.400 --> 00:23:22.710
to support its implementation,

00:23:22.710 --> 00:23:25.530
how am I gonna get
everyone else to support it

00:23:25.530 --> 00:23:29.460
who feels stigmatized
'cause these illnesses?

00:23:29.460 --> 00:23:32.430
So we're finally getting some attention,

00:23:32.430 --> 00:23:35.847
but, Dr. Addy, it's
because corporate America

00:23:35.847 --> 00:23:40.847
is nervous that the great resignation,

00:23:41.760 --> 00:23:45.480
and the competition for talent means

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:47.940
that they better do
something on mental health

00:23:47.940 --> 00:23:51.330
because these Gen Xers, and others,

00:23:51.330 --> 00:23:55.470
these young workforce
is demanding no less.

00:23:55.470 --> 00:23:58.290
And they know intuitively,

00:23:58.290 --> 00:24:00.360
that if they don't
invest in mental health,

00:24:00.360 --> 00:24:04.140
not only is it a cost on
their total health care costs

00:24:04.140 --> 00:24:05.220
in their workforce,

00:24:05.220 --> 00:24:08.493
but it's a cost to their
economic productivity,

00:24:10.110 --> 00:24:15.000
but still today, Dr. Addy,
we have not wrapped our arms

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:20.000
around the full economic
implications of poor mental health,

00:24:21.270 --> 00:24:25.680
and the full benefits of real
good integrated mental health

00:24:25.680 --> 00:24:27.216
into the rest of physical health care.

00:24:27.216 --> 00:24:32.216
And until we do, it's gonna
be still a push and pull

00:24:32.250 --> 00:24:35.670
to get corporations to do
what's in their self-interest,

00:24:35.670 --> 00:24:40.670
to get insurance companies to
get past this gatekeeper idea

00:24:41.040 --> 00:24:43.840
that all they need to be doing
in this world is limiting

00:24:45.180 --> 00:24:49.680
cost and access, which is
counterproductive in every way

00:24:49.680 --> 00:24:51.990
as we know from the early data.

00:24:51.990 --> 00:24:54.780
So it's still a long ways to go.

00:24:54.780 --> 00:24:57.579
- Yeah, a long haul, and
I appreciate, I mean,

00:24:57.579 --> 00:25:00.360
the way you've given us
just the full context.

00:25:00.360 --> 00:25:01.980
I mean, even from the early work,

00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:04.238
and the strategy involved in that.

00:25:04.238 --> 00:25:07.260
and how you had to partner with others

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:08.850
to really move things
along in a certain way,

00:25:08.850 --> 00:25:10.560
but it sounds like you're
also being very savvy

00:25:10.560 --> 00:25:13.230
about the motivation behind that as well.

00:25:13.230 --> 00:25:15.090
And you were kind of touching on this.

00:25:15.090 --> 00:25:17.460
Do you feel like with everything
that's happened with COVID,

00:25:17.460 --> 00:25:19.860
is there any shift whatsoever,

00:25:19.860 --> 00:25:21.180
or any greater acknowledgement

00:25:21.180 --> 00:25:22.710
that you think will help
move things forward,

00:25:22.710 --> 00:25:26.220
or is it still just all
these aspects of resistance

00:25:26.220 --> 00:25:27.053
that you've talked about

00:25:27.053 --> 00:25:29.140
that are still kind of
the major roadblocks?

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:33.120
- So the 800 pound gorilla is that

00:25:33.120 --> 00:25:36.990
we don't have enough workers
to meet the current crisis.

00:25:36.990 --> 00:25:40.080
We're not gonna have near
the workers we need to meet

00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:42.270
the future mental health crisis.

00:25:42.270 --> 00:25:43.351
We've got a tsunami.

00:25:43.351 --> 00:25:46.530
The racial pandemic, the COVID pandemic,

00:25:46.530 --> 00:25:48.450
the economic dislocation,

00:25:48.450 --> 00:25:53.010
moving into more
technology-based economies,

00:25:53.010 --> 00:25:58.010
the dislocation of workers,
the global implications,

00:25:58.170 --> 00:26:03.170
Ukraine, the virus, the environment.

00:26:03.660 --> 00:26:08.430
I mean, it's just no shortage
and we're really not using,

00:26:08.430 --> 00:26:11.400
as I said earlier our prefrontal cortex.

00:26:11.400 --> 00:26:16.129
We're really acting out
of our mammalian brain.

00:26:16.129 --> 00:26:18.718
Our amygdala is in full flight.

00:26:18.718 --> 00:26:23.520
I really think we need
neuro-literacy in this country.

00:26:23.520 --> 00:26:26.700
In other words, I think a generation ago,

00:26:26.700 --> 00:26:30.090
corporate America pushed STEM education.

00:26:30.090 --> 00:26:32.820
They didn't think we
had enough scientists,

00:26:32.820 --> 00:26:35.550
technologists, mathematicians.

00:26:35.550 --> 00:26:37.260
China was eating our lunch.

00:26:37.260 --> 00:26:39.210
The rest of the world was advancing.

00:26:39.210 --> 00:26:40.440
So corporate America said,

00:26:40.440 --> 00:26:42.840
oh, we gotta teach this in our schools.

00:26:42.840 --> 00:26:44.370
And that's where we ended up shifting

00:26:44.370 --> 00:26:46.347
our educational paradigm.

00:26:46.347 --> 00:26:50.580
We're gonna need to do the
same on neuro-literacy.

00:26:50.580 --> 00:26:53.340
And the reason I'm saying
it is if we don't have

00:26:53.340 --> 00:26:56.700
the next generation of
workers able to manage,

00:26:56.700 --> 00:26:59.438
and cope with stress,

00:26:59.438 --> 00:27:02.506
we're not gonna have a
productive workforce.

00:27:02.506 --> 00:27:06.810
And I think this existential
crisis in corporate America

00:27:06.810 --> 00:27:08.193
with the great resignation,

00:27:08.193 --> 00:27:10.410
I think it's got them thinking,

00:27:10.410 --> 00:27:12.390
oh my God, like, what are we gonna do?

00:27:12.390 --> 00:27:15.300
Like this whole new generation,
they're not subscribing

00:27:15.300 --> 00:27:18.360
to the old traditional paradigm of work,

00:27:18.360 --> 00:27:21.420
and what is it gonna mean
to have them work for us?

00:27:21.420 --> 00:27:22.500
How are we gonna make sure

00:27:22.500 --> 00:27:27.500
they're optimal in their
capacity and we retain them?

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:30.330
I just think that it's gonna now require

00:27:30.330 --> 00:27:32.700
that they really go back and think,

00:27:32.700 --> 00:27:36.810
how are we producing people in this world

00:27:36.810 --> 00:27:40.590
in a way where they can
be much more productive?

00:27:40.590 --> 00:27:43.620
And it's basically
about a prevention model

00:27:43.620 --> 00:27:46.020
because if we don't have that,

00:27:46.020 --> 00:27:50.430
then stress as you know is
the environmental factor

00:27:50.430 --> 00:27:54.480
that can tip off the
genetic predisposition

00:27:54.480 --> 00:27:56.370
to all array of mental illnesses,

00:27:56.370 --> 00:27:58.440
and addictions particularly.

00:27:58.440 --> 00:28:03.440
So the key is to interrupt
that stress where possible

00:28:04.260 --> 00:28:07.230
through tools that people can use

00:28:07.230 --> 00:28:09.390
so that their predisposition,

00:28:09.390 --> 00:28:11.457
if they have a greater
vulnerability to depression,

00:28:11.457 --> 00:28:14.670
and anxiety, or an eating
disorder, or alcoholism,

00:28:14.670 --> 00:28:16.950
that they can acknowledge that,

00:28:16.950 --> 00:28:19.410
and then intervene and do these things

00:28:19.410 --> 00:28:20.398
that we're just talking about,

00:28:20.398 --> 00:28:23.040
go outside, connect with family,

00:28:23.040 --> 00:28:27.288
understand the value of
interpersonal relationships

00:28:27.288 --> 00:28:31.200
to their own mental health, so, yeah.

00:28:31.200 --> 00:28:34.140
I think there's a lot to
wrap our arms around here.

00:28:34.140 --> 00:28:35.880
- Yeah, well, so many important pieces

00:28:35.880 --> 00:28:37.440
of what you've pulled out too as well.

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:40.036
I mean, as you acknowledged
just the workforce shortage,

00:28:40.036 --> 00:28:43.380
but then also just the
need for the workforce

00:28:43.380 --> 00:28:45.540
to be infiltrated in a sense.

00:28:45.540 --> 00:28:46.890
And obviously that can be a challenge

00:28:46.890 --> 00:28:47.760
when there aren't enough,

00:28:47.760 --> 00:28:50.094
but I'm thinking in terms of
clinicians and researchers,

00:28:50.094 --> 00:28:51.930
and just being out in the community,

00:28:51.930 --> 00:28:55.163
and helping with this literacy,
as it were so to speak.

00:28:55.163 --> 00:28:56.790
I mean, all the just different things

00:28:56.790 --> 00:28:59.070
that you've highlighted of
all the needs that are there,

00:28:59.070 --> 00:29:01.260
that were things really
need to kind of push,

00:29:01.260 --> 00:29:02.335
and move forward.

00:29:02.335 --> 00:29:04.050
So going back to something we mentioned

00:29:04.050 --> 00:29:06.000
at the top of the episode, too,

00:29:06.000 --> 00:29:07.950
even just thinking about hope
in the midst of everything,

00:29:07.950 --> 00:29:10.500
because you've obviously had some things

00:29:10.500 --> 00:29:11.700
that have been put into place

00:29:11.700 --> 00:29:12.960
that aren't necessarily being implicated

00:29:12.960 --> 00:29:14.250
as much as they should,

00:29:14.250 --> 00:29:15.690
but at the same time you're continuing

00:29:15.690 --> 00:29:17.700
to move things forward
even in the midst of that.

00:29:17.700 --> 00:29:19.320
So I'm thinking about all
the different initiatives

00:29:19.320 --> 00:29:20.700
that you've been involved in.

00:29:20.700 --> 00:29:22.080
I'm thinking about One Mind.

00:29:22.080 --> 00:29:22.913
I'm thinking about

00:29:22.913 --> 00:29:25.470
the Kennedy Satcher Center
for Mental Health Equity

00:29:25.470 --> 00:29:26.760
at the Morehouse School of Medicine,

00:29:26.760 --> 00:29:28.906
which seemed like it was
pioneering in many ways,

00:29:28.906 --> 00:29:32.520
speaking about and moving
forward in those aspects.

00:29:32.520 --> 00:29:35.670
I'm thinking about the work
that you've been involved in,

00:29:35.670 --> 00:29:37.470
and all that's going on with the Wit,

00:29:37.470 --> 00:29:40.186
and the United Behavioral
Health case as well.

00:29:40.186 --> 00:29:43.170
I'm wondering even in the
midst of the challenges,

00:29:43.170 --> 00:29:46.080
how do you see hope in the midst
of what you're trying to do

00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:46.920
in some of these efforts?

00:29:46.920 --> 00:29:48.960
Because I've also heard
you say things like,

00:29:48.960 --> 00:29:49.920
and you've alluded to that here,

00:29:49.920 --> 00:29:51.900
that we need to get to a new normal.

00:29:51.900 --> 00:29:53.826
How do you balance those two?

00:29:53.826 --> 00:29:55.950
This is a little bit
of a personal question,

00:29:55.950 --> 00:29:59.010
but also a little bit of
a programmatic question

00:29:59.010 --> 00:30:01.440
in terms of how you
just go about this work

00:30:01.440 --> 00:30:04.613
on a day to day basis with
all that needs to be done?

00:30:04.613 --> 00:30:09.480
- Well, I get on my knees in the morning.

00:30:09.480 --> 00:30:12.690
I try to humble myself and think about

00:30:12.690 --> 00:30:16.260
what my greatest purpose and utility is.

00:30:16.260 --> 00:30:19.800
And I know as I mentioned earlier,

00:30:19.800 --> 00:30:22.623
I may not have had the smartest

00:30:22.623 --> 00:30:25.274
best grade point average or the best this.

00:30:25.274 --> 00:30:28.530
I've been blessed with
this family narrative

00:30:28.530 --> 00:30:30.480
that I think is very conducive

00:30:30.480 --> 00:30:33.780
to pulling these different
strings together,

00:30:33.780 --> 00:30:35.490
and creating a narrative.

00:30:35.490 --> 00:30:37.980
I think we've missed the narrative in this

00:30:37.980 --> 00:30:40.200
because we have addiction over here,

00:30:40.200 --> 00:30:41.610
and mental illness over here,

00:30:41.610 --> 00:30:44.190
and then people with
mental wellness issues

00:30:44.190 --> 00:30:46.350
aren't seeing themselves in either bucket.

00:30:46.350 --> 00:30:48.150
We just don't have this holistic picture

00:30:48.150 --> 00:30:50.460
that this is good for everybody.

00:30:50.460 --> 00:30:52.950
The Green Berets have more mental health

00:30:52.950 --> 00:30:54.900
than any other branch of the service.

00:30:54.900 --> 00:30:56.213
And how do I know that?

00:30:56.213 --> 00:30:58.110
Because John F. Kennedy

00:30:58.110 --> 00:30:59.850
awarded the wearing of the green beret,

00:30:59.850 --> 00:31:01.560
and I went and rededicated

00:31:01.560 --> 00:31:03.780
the Fort Bragg Special Warfare School

00:31:03.780 --> 00:31:05.730
named for John F. Kennedy.

00:31:05.730 --> 00:31:08.850
And it was at that, that
General Hugh Shelton,

00:31:08.850 --> 00:31:11.010
first chairman of the
Joint Chiefs of Staff,

00:31:11.010 --> 00:31:12.337
came up to me and said,

00:31:12.337 --> 00:31:14.190
"Congressman, we have more mental health

00:31:14.190 --> 00:31:16.620
for our Green Berets than any
other branch of the service."

00:31:16.620 --> 00:31:18.660
And I knew he had seen my CV.

00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:21.660
So I thought to myself, well,
he's just being political.

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:23.610
So I said, "General,
listen, these Green Berets,

00:31:23.610 --> 00:31:24.630
they don't need mental health.

00:31:24.630 --> 00:31:27.960
They jump out of airplanes,
and they hit the water,

00:31:27.960 --> 00:31:32.010
and they swim under water for
five miles without breathing.

00:31:32.010 --> 00:31:35.940
And then they hit the beach
and they speak six languages,

00:31:35.940 --> 00:31:37.380
and they take out their target,

00:31:37.380 --> 00:31:39.213
and they're reading to
their kids by dinnertime.

00:31:39.213 --> 00:31:41.970
They don't need mental health."

00:31:41.970 --> 00:31:43.237
And General Shelton said,

00:31:43.237 --> 00:31:45.270
"Congressman, you don't understand."

00:31:45.270 --> 00:31:48.900
And I was like, taken back, like, "What?"

00:31:48.900 --> 00:31:49.777
And he said,

00:31:49.777 --> 00:31:53.550
"We look at mental health
as a force multiplier."

00:31:53.550 --> 00:31:55.830
And I thought to myself, brilliant.

00:31:55.830 --> 00:31:57.840
I had never heard that before.

00:31:57.840 --> 00:32:01.650
I just thought of mental
health as being about deficits.

00:32:01.650 --> 00:32:03.300
I never thought about mental health

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:05.130
being the glass was half full.

00:32:05.130 --> 00:32:09.630
Like you mean people who are
strong also need mental health?

00:32:09.630 --> 00:32:11.070
And so I asked him about it.

00:32:11.070 --> 00:32:13.522
He said, "Yeah, we need
our special operators

00:32:13.522 --> 00:32:18.260
to work at their optimal full capacity."

00:32:18.260 --> 00:32:22.560
And so I was like, that makes sense.

00:32:22.560 --> 00:32:23.880
And my point is,

00:32:23.880 --> 00:32:27.930
is that that and then my
family history of civil rights

00:32:27.930 --> 00:32:29.460
got me this perspective,

00:32:29.460 --> 00:32:32.319
like, when David Satcher
who I'd worked with,

00:32:32.319 --> 00:32:34.078
when he was surgeon general,

00:32:34.078 --> 00:32:36.900
was the first surgeon general
to do a mental health report,

00:32:36.900 --> 00:32:38.887
called on me after I left Congress said,

00:32:38.887 --> 00:32:43.380
"Do you wanna be part of
Morehouse School of Medicine?"

00:32:43.380 --> 00:32:45.030
I mean, first I had to say yes,

00:32:45.030 --> 00:32:48.720
'cause David Satcher was
in jail with Dr. King,

00:32:48.720 --> 00:32:51.780
amongst a lot of young student
protestors at the time.

00:32:51.780 --> 00:32:54.270
And to my view of growing up

00:32:54.270 --> 00:32:56.220
they're part of our founding fathers.

00:32:56.220 --> 00:32:57.930
They're the ones who laid the groundwork

00:32:57.930 --> 00:32:59.280
for a modern-day America

00:32:59.280 --> 00:33:03.370
where we passed the Civil
Rights Act, Voting Rights Act,

00:33:03.370 --> 00:33:05.490
Fair Housing Act, Fair Employment,

00:33:05.490 --> 00:33:09.270
all of which laid the ground
for progress in this country

00:33:09.270 --> 00:33:11.070
for more Americans to be included

00:33:11.070 --> 00:33:12.903
in the circle of opportunity,

00:33:13.770 --> 00:33:16.980
as opposed to just being
an elite selective society

00:33:16.980 --> 00:33:20.730
of just a few people
who had all the power.

00:33:20.730 --> 00:33:22.830
So I said, "Yes."

00:33:22.830 --> 00:33:27.830
That was 10 years before George Floyd.

00:33:28.050 --> 00:33:33.000
I had no idea because
as most white Americans,

00:33:33.000 --> 00:33:36.000
like, this thing that we
now saw with George Floyd

00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:38.121
was happening every single day in America.

00:33:38.121 --> 00:33:40.860
We didn't have it captured on video,

00:33:40.860 --> 00:33:43.071
like it was so painfully captured

00:33:43.071 --> 00:33:46.170
when George Floyd was killed.

00:33:46.170 --> 00:33:50.460
And since then there's been
229 additional Black people

00:33:50.460 --> 00:33:52.830
who have been killed
at the hands of police

00:33:52.830 --> 00:33:55.080
since George Floyd.

00:33:55.080 --> 00:33:56.800
I only know that

00:33:59.520 --> 00:34:04.470
tragically to say because I've
been pulled into Morehouse

00:34:04.470 --> 00:34:06.990
because of Dr. Satcher,

00:34:06.990 --> 00:34:09.150
his relationship with my family,

00:34:09.150 --> 00:34:11.010
my relationship with him,

00:34:11.010 --> 00:34:14.520
to have a little bit broader perspective

00:34:14.520 --> 00:34:16.380
than I would have otherwise.

00:34:16.380 --> 00:34:18.930
And so I've been the
one who's learned about

00:34:18.930 --> 00:34:21.510
what is vicarious trauma.

00:34:21.510 --> 00:34:26.010
And when others tell me,
well, it's like seeing

00:34:26.010 --> 00:34:30.612
your family members killed
routinely on television

00:34:30.612 --> 00:34:35.363
in these photos because of
the color of their skin.

00:34:35.363 --> 00:34:39.090
I have to go into my closet,
one of my friends said,

00:34:39.090 --> 00:34:41.910
and take out all the hoodies in my closet

00:34:41.910 --> 00:34:45.330
because I'm worried my
son is gonna be killed.

00:34:45.330 --> 00:34:49.053
I'm saying to myself,
that's not my experience.

00:34:49.890 --> 00:34:51.750
And then it dawns on me.

00:34:51.750 --> 00:34:55.290
I have a moral obligation
to do everything I can

00:34:55.290 --> 00:34:57.090
because if that were me,

00:34:57.090 --> 00:35:01.200
I can't imagine the rage that I would have

00:35:01.200 --> 00:35:05.034
if my family members
were constantly stopped

00:35:05.034 --> 00:35:06.934
because they were suspected

00:35:06.934 --> 00:35:10.050
of committing some type of crime,

00:35:10.050 --> 00:35:11.910
because that's how we've racialized

00:35:11.910 --> 00:35:14.130
our criminal justice system.

00:35:14.130 --> 00:35:17.280
In any event, all of this, Dr. Addy,

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:20.910
is not out of my personal choice.

00:35:20.910 --> 00:35:25.890
I have been born in an
environment that has worked on me,

00:35:25.890 --> 00:35:30.187
and shaped me, which
I owe my existence to.

00:35:31.080 --> 00:35:34.440
So when I think about
what do we need to do,

00:35:34.440 --> 00:35:37.603
I think about trying to tie this together

00:35:37.603 --> 00:35:42.603
as a modern-day medical
version of civil rights

00:35:44.133 --> 00:35:46.380
because if you think about it,

00:35:46.380 --> 00:35:50.340
mental health is separate and unequal.

00:35:50.340 --> 00:35:54.120
And you think about people who
suffer from mental illness,

00:35:54.120 --> 00:35:56.563
marginalized, and by the way,

00:35:56.563 --> 00:36:00.051
like Black people shot inordinately

00:36:00.051 --> 00:36:04.230
during emergency response,
vis-à-vis the general population.

00:36:04.230 --> 00:36:08.100
And like with communities
of color also incarcerated

00:36:08.100 --> 00:36:10.262
at much bigger numbers, right?

00:36:10.262 --> 00:36:13.560
So you've got a lot of the same dynamics.

00:36:13.560 --> 00:36:16.620
I never would've made those connections

00:36:16.620 --> 00:36:19.260
had I not had my life experiences.

00:36:19.260 --> 00:36:20.880
So when I say I get down on my knees,

00:36:20.880 --> 00:36:24.510
I think about, well, what's my job?

00:36:24.510 --> 00:36:27.060
It's to move with what I've been given.

00:36:27.060 --> 00:36:30.393
And I've been given these
ways of looking at the world,

00:36:31.740 --> 00:36:36.240
which help me in my view
advance this advocacy.

00:36:36.240 --> 00:36:38.040
And by the way, I can advance it

00:36:38.040 --> 00:36:41.430
because I got this family name.

00:36:41.430 --> 00:36:44.958
I've got connections to
the corridors of power.

00:36:44.958 --> 00:36:48.570
I can bring attention and I
don't have to feel diminished

00:36:48.570 --> 00:36:52.290
because I also have
depression and anxiety.

00:36:52.290 --> 00:36:54.963
I also have drug addiction.

00:36:57.360 --> 00:36:59.520
Whereas, if I didn't have my last name,

00:36:59.520 --> 00:37:03.690
maybe I wouldn't be so racing
to the door to be an advocate,

00:37:03.690 --> 00:37:05.610
because let's be honest,

00:37:05.610 --> 00:37:07.440
if people were just to
look at me as someone

00:37:07.440 --> 00:37:10.615
who's in recovery from a
mental illness or addiction,

00:37:10.615 --> 00:37:13.740
I could easily be dismissed.

00:37:13.740 --> 00:37:16.110
So I feel like I have,

00:37:16.110 --> 00:37:18.526
and by the way, I spend
a better part of my day,

00:37:18.526 --> 00:37:21.180
helping my friends get connected to care

00:37:21.180 --> 00:37:23.160
just in the microworld.

00:37:23.160 --> 00:37:26.795
And also calling up my friends
from insurance companies

00:37:26.795 --> 00:37:28.800
saying, you gotta cover this person.

00:37:28.800 --> 00:37:30.724
It's your legal
obligation, and they do it,

00:37:30.724 --> 00:37:35.724
but that's only because that's
what God has given me to do

00:37:35.910 --> 00:37:38.280
with what I have been given in my life,

00:37:38.280 --> 00:37:42.180
so finding purpose to me
is the way to find hope.

00:37:42.180 --> 00:37:43.650
- Yeah, that's so powerful.

00:37:43.650 --> 00:37:45.496
I mean, so much of what you said

00:37:45.496 --> 00:37:48.390
resonates on so many levels.

00:37:48.390 --> 00:37:50.490
I mean, obviously, it's sobering.

00:37:50.490 --> 00:37:52.980
At the same time it's also refreshing.

00:37:52.980 --> 00:37:54.720
And just hearing you talk through that,

00:37:54.720 --> 00:37:57.000
just seeing all the
ways that you've really

00:37:57.000 --> 00:37:59.490
just walked in community with others.

00:37:59.490 --> 00:38:00.753
So that sounds like you're
not coming in saying

00:38:00.753 --> 00:38:03.780
that you have all this
understanding already,

00:38:03.780 --> 00:38:07.254
but you're partnering with
people who are trusted,

00:38:07.254 --> 00:38:09.030
who are colleagues, who are friends,

00:38:09.030 --> 00:38:10.500
even as you talked about Dr. Satcher,

00:38:10.500 --> 00:38:12.150
and learning from people's experiences,

00:38:12.150 --> 00:38:14.100
and just the way that you have been able

00:38:14.100 --> 00:38:17.280
to incorporate that, and get
that level of understanding.

00:38:17.280 --> 00:38:18.630
Granted, you didn't walk
through it yourself,

00:38:18.630 --> 00:38:20.970
but just even as you related
to the rage that you would feel

00:38:20.970 --> 00:38:22.180
if you were in that similar situation,

00:38:22.180 --> 00:38:24.535
and I would say that's so important.

00:38:24.535 --> 00:38:26.190
And even as you're sharing,

00:38:26.190 --> 00:38:28.146
I'm still kind of just
processing and taking in all,

00:38:28.146 --> 00:38:30.330
again, the passion that's there,

00:38:30.330 --> 00:38:33.360
and the ways that you've
really been adamant about

00:38:33.360 --> 00:38:34.740
putting it into practice,

00:38:34.740 --> 00:38:36.060
and making sure that it's something

00:38:36.060 --> 00:38:39.180
that can really help us shift and move

00:38:39.180 --> 00:38:42.300
how we go about these things
on a day to day basis.

00:38:42.300 --> 00:38:45.000
So just, again, gratitude for the ways

00:38:45.000 --> 00:38:47.730
that you've acknowledged
the role that you have,

00:38:47.730 --> 00:38:50.100
the opportunity that you have
in walking alongside others

00:38:50.100 --> 00:38:52.800
to really make sure that
that comes to fruition

00:38:52.800 --> 00:38:54.100
in so many different ways.

00:38:55.260 --> 00:38:57.732
- Yeah, no, I mean, of course,

00:38:57.732 --> 00:39:01.140
I've voted against
Defense of Marriage Act.

00:39:01.140 --> 00:39:06.063
I was signing on everything,
civil rights, LGBTQ.

00:39:06.993 --> 00:39:09.300
It was politically the
right thing to do, right?

00:39:09.300 --> 00:39:10.863
As for my mindset.

00:39:12.073 --> 00:39:14.160
I have a family member,

00:39:14.160 --> 00:39:17.130
a daughter now who's
grappling with identity.

00:39:17.130 --> 00:39:20.943
I could never have imagined
why the work that I did,

00:39:20.943 --> 00:39:25.943
a decade two ago around this
issue could come home, right?

00:39:28.261 --> 00:39:31.101
And I try to tell people,

00:39:31.101 --> 00:39:33.390
even if you haven't had a mental illness,

00:39:33.390 --> 00:39:35.130
or addiction in your family,

00:39:35.130 --> 00:39:36.927
it's gonna come home to you at some point.

00:39:36.927 --> 00:39:40.370
And what kind of world do
you want for your loved one?

00:39:40.370 --> 00:39:42.810
Do you wanna have to wait six months

00:39:42.810 --> 00:39:44.820
before you can see Dr. Addy?

00:39:44.820 --> 00:39:47.880
Because that's right now what it takes

00:39:47.880 --> 00:39:50.560
to get a really good psychiatric hearing,

00:39:53.970 --> 00:39:58.892
and then what kind of therapeutic

00:39:58.892 --> 00:40:02.580
are you gonna just be
shipped off to some kind of

00:40:02.580 --> 00:40:04.380
one size fits all approach,

00:40:04.380 --> 00:40:08.880
or is your loved one gonna
kind of get the curated

00:40:08.880 --> 00:40:10.470
best evidence-based treatment

00:40:10.470 --> 00:40:12.930
for their particular diagnosis?

00:40:12.930 --> 00:40:17.930
So I was fortunate to get
great psychiatric care myself,

00:40:18.120 --> 00:40:21.363
but I didn't get cognitive
behavioral therapy, Dr. Addy.

00:40:23.076 --> 00:40:28.076
I'm very much interested in
getting CBT more adopted,

00:40:28.560 --> 00:40:31.440
just generally, and incentivizing that,

00:40:31.440 --> 00:40:33.465
and having kind of an Angie's List,

00:40:33.465 --> 00:40:37.534
and a match.com set up so
that if I need to get care,

00:40:37.534 --> 00:40:39.880
and if my daughter

00:40:40.770 --> 00:40:44.486
has to get some skills
with eating disorders

00:40:44.486 --> 00:40:46.440
that she's not going to someone

00:40:46.440 --> 00:40:48.990
who's just good on depression and anxiety,

00:40:48.990 --> 00:40:50.970
even though she has
depression and anxiety,

00:40:50.970 --> 00:40:53.250
like, what is it that she'll need?

00:40:53.250 --> 00:40:57.030
I want therapists that are
gonna be good for her, right?

00:40:57.030 --> 00:41:01.980
And right now she'll be lucky
she gets anybody, right?

00:41:01.980 --> 00:41:02.813
- Yeah.

00:41:02.813 --> 00:41:04.200
- Not the least of which is someone

00:41:04.200 --> 00:41:07.320
who can actually help her with
her particular challenges,

00:41:07.320 --> 00:41:10.500
or someone who could
help me with mine, so.

00:41:10.500 --> 00:41:12.810
We need to really ramp up

00:41:12.810 --> 00:41:15.120
the delivery of evidence-based treatment,

00:41:15.120 --> 00:41:18.210
in addition to doing
what the Parity Law says,

00:41:18.210 --> 00:41:19.473
and that is pay for it.

00:41:20.340 --> 00:41:22.110
Obviously, chicken and egg,

00:41:22.110 --> 00:41:24.372
like, you gotta put a
lot more money in this,

00:41:24.372 --> 00:41:27.270
but my hope is that when
we put more money in it,

00:41:27.270 --> 00:41:30.972
we also demand kind of greater
outcomes at the same time,

00:41:30.972 --> 00:41:33.090
because we have a double problem.

00:41:33.090 --> 00:41:36.120
We have a problem of
accessing mental health,

00:41:36.120 --> 00:41:37.290
but then we have another problem

00:41:37.290 --> 00:41:41.580
of accessing quality mental
health, and we need to do both.

00:41:41.580 --> 00:41:45.450
And that, by the way, takes payers,

00:41:45.450 --> 00:41:47.892
and then, of course, it takes providers.

00:41:47.892 --> 00:41:49.469
so it's not an either/or.

00:41:49.469 --> 00:41:51.223
We need both of them
to be together in this.

00:41:51.223 --> 00:41:53.610
- Yeah, it's so challenging
as you mentioned.

00:41:53.610 --> 00:41:56.030
With some of the guests that
we've had on this program too,

00:41:56.030 --> 00:41:58.611
we always talk about some of
the severe mental illnesses,

00:41:58.611 --> 00:42:01.320
and things around suicide prevention,

00:42:01.320 --> 00:42:02.790
as you are familiar with from your work,

00:42:02.790 --> 00:42:04.770
just ways that we, actually,

00:42:04.770 --> 00:42:06.540
in some ways need to rebuild the system,

00:42:06.540 --> 00:42:07.950
but what can we do in the meantime

00:42:07.950 --> 00:42:09.683
to really help things move forward?

00:42:09.683 --> 00:42:12.750
And so, again, just know that
the work that you're doing

00:42:12.750 --> 00:42:14.577
is so important in so many different ways.

00:42:14.577 --> 00:42:15.780
And I'm wondering if you,

00:42:15.780 --> 00:42:18.210
also, just talk a little
bit about the ongoing case,

00:42:18.210 --> 00:42:20.880
and what really needs to
shift in ways that you think

00:42:20.880 --> 00:42:23.220
that we can do that to have an impact,

00:42:23.220 --> 00:42:26.580
even with the ongoing
case I alluded to earlier?

00:42:26.580 --> 00:42:28.230
- Yeah, well, we need advocacy.

00:42:28.230 --> 00:42:29.700
That's the big missing piece.

00:42:29.700 --> 00:42:32.070
Like it's not as if I don't have

00:42:32.070 --> 00:42:36.480
the whole matrix of
policies already enumerated

00:42:36.480 --> 00:42:40.320
by every subcommittee in
the United States Congress,

00:42:40.320 --> 00:42:41.900
like we've never had before.

00:42:41.900 --> 00:42:43.380
I mean, this is not

00:42:43.380 --> 00:42:45.180
just the purview of the Health Committee.

00:42:45.180 --> 00:42:47.670
It's the purview of the Housing Committee,

00:42:47.670 --> 00:42:51.103
the Labor Committee,
the Education Committee.

00:42:51.103 --> 00:42:54.600
And my point is, is that it's
a comprehensive approach,

00:42:54.600 --> 00:42:58.080
but what we need are we
need people to sign up.

00:42:58.080 --> 00:43:00.360
Some people will be interested
in the supportive housing,

00:43:00.360 --> 00:43:01.650
and the community model.

00:43:01.650 --> 00:43:03.750
Others will be about the neuroscience,

00:43:03.750 --> 00:43:06.600
and coming up with new
pharmaceutical interventions.

00:43:06.600 --> 00:43:08.370
Others will be about expanding,

00:43:08.370 --> 00:43:10.260
and getting rid of the IMD exclusion,

00:43:10.260 --> 00:43:13.718
so people can get adequate
inpatient access to care,

00:43:13.718 --> 00:43:17.010
expanding the telemental health.

00:43:17.010 --> 00:43:21.840
The point is, is that we just
need an advocacy movement,

00:43:21.840 --> 00:43:23.580
and we need to look at

00:43:23.580 --> 00:43:25.650
what the Chamber of Commerce has done,

00:43:25.650 --> 00:43:30.330
the League of Conservation
Voters, the AFL-CIO.

00:43:30.330 --> 00:43:33.930
You could name your advocacy
group, they've got it all.

00:43:33.930 --> 00:43:38.220
They've got what is the
uniform kind of agenda

00:43:38.220 --> 00:43:42.210
that unites all of the affiliates
within organized labor?

00:43:42.210 --> 00:43:43.860
Well, there's different types of unions,

00:43:43.860 --> 00:43:44.970
but they all kind of agree,

00:43:44.970 --> 00:43:49.970
well, we need kind of minimum
wage, fair labor, pensions.

00:43:50.160 --> 00:43:52.260
We need decent health care.

00:43:52.260 --> 00:43:54.153
You've got some standards, right?

00:43:55.590 --> 00:43:59.790
And in environment it's
League of Conservation Voters.

00:43:59.790 --> 00:44:02.460
Yeah, some of us are interested in air,

00:44:02.460 --> 00:44:05.960
as osmotic water, land.

00:44:05.960 --> 00:44:10.080
The whole idea is, yeah, but
we have a unified agenda.

00:44:10.080 --> 00:44:11.250
These are the big issues.

00:44:11.250 --> 00:44:13.360
Like we don't have that
in mental health yet.

00:44:13.360 --> 00:44:16.950
Parity for years was kind
of the singular issue

00:44:16.950 --> 00:44:18.390
that united the whole community,

00:44:18.390 --> 00:44:20.480
whether you had addiction
or mental illness,

00:44:20.480 --> 00:44:23.851
whether you were a
psychiatrist, or psychologist.

00:44:23.851 --> 00:44:27.180
We need to bake this
so that everybody sees

00:44:27.180 --> 00:44:28.080
their own advantage.

00:44:28.080 --> 00:44:30.090
And we get, as I said,

00:44:30.090 --> 00:44:34.350
the 28 million Americans
in long-term recovery

00:44:34.350 --> 00:44:38.700
who were, quote, anonymous
out of these church basements,

00:44:38.700 --> 00:44:42.090
and understanding that our founder Bill W.

00:44:42.090 --> 00:44:44.373
testified in front of Congress,

00:44:45.360 --> 00:44:49.110
that you can say you're a
person in long-term recovery

00:44:49.110 --> 00:44:52.380
without having to acknowledge
the exact 12-step group

00:44:52.380 --> 00:44:53.610
that you belong to,

00:44:53.610 --> 00:44:58.170
and still maintain the
11th tradition of privacy

00:44:58.170 --> 00:45:02.400
at the level of press,
newspaper, and radio.

00:45:02.400 --> 00:45:06.900
So we just don't have a
sophistication in advocacy

00:45:06.900 --> 00:45:10.050
that lends itself to
ramping these issues up.

00:45:10.050 --> 00:45:13.470
Because my wife ran for
Congress this last cycle,

00:45:13.470 --> 00:45:18.470
I could not get a list of
families impacted by this illness.

00:45:19.637 --> 00:45:22.800
I couldn't get a list of
all the paraprofessionals

00:45:22.800 --> 00:45:24.840
who treat these illnesses.

00:45:24.840 --> 00:45:28.080
I couldn't get, I mean, I could
just keep going on and on.

00:45:28.080 --> 00:45:33.080
Whereas, if I wanted to
reach working families,

00:45:33.840 --> 00:45:38.010
I had every single union mailing list.

00:45:38.010 --> 00:45:39.570
I would make a phone call.

00:45:39.570 --> 00:45:42.510
There would be an automatic rally.

00:45:42.510 --> 00:45:45.420
If my wife went out to do canvasing,

00:45:45.420 --> 00:45:49.860
she'd be accompanied with 50
other of her fellow teachers.

00:45:49.860 --> 00:45:52.410
Like we need that in mental
health and addiction.

00:45:52.410 --> 00:45:54.960
And if we don't get that, Dr. Addy,

00:45:54.960 --> 00:45:57.540
we can have all these great policies,

00:45:57.540 --> 00:45:59.070
but they're not gonna translate

00:45:59.070 --> 00:46:01.020
because we don't put
the money behind them,

00:46:01.020 --> 00:46:03.570
and we don't put the bodies behind them.

00:46:03.570 --> 00:46:05.520
And that's what we need the most.

00:46:05.520 --> 00:46:08.340
- Yeah, just to change
all the way around from,

00:46:08.340 --> 00:46:10.080
like you said, to making
sure that it's, I mean,

00:46:10.080 --> 00:46:11.220
I'm thinking back to what you shared about

00:46:11.220 --> 00:46:12.300
the Green Berets, too, it seemed like

00:46:12.300 --> 00:46:14.820
it was baked into every
aspect of what they did.

00:46:14.820 --> 00:46:17.940
And that was really where it
started and where it ended,

00:46:17.940 --> 00:46:19.050
and how it just wrapped around.

00:46:19.050 --> 00:46:20.190
So as much as we need to do that

00:46:20.190 --> 00:46:21.990
on all these different components,

00:46:21.990 --> 00:46:23.940
as you mentioned on all these
different subcommittees,

00:46:23.940 --> 00:46:24.970
on all these different topics,

00:46:24.970 --> 00:46:28.200
so important in so many ways.

00:46:28.200 --> 00:46:30.617
I'm glad you brought your wife up as well.

00:46:30.617 --> 00:46:33.900
Also, thinking about her role
previously as an educator,

00:46:33.900 --> 00:46:37.050
and how important that
component is to even

00:46:37.050 --> 00:46:39.390
as you were talking about some of the CBT,

00:46:39.390 --> 00:46:41.610
I have some clinical
psychology colleagues,

00:46:41.610 --> 00:46:43.860
who've talked about how they
love to see some of those

00:46:43.860 --> 00:46:46.980
principles integrated into
the schools from day one,

00:46:46.980 --> 00:46:49.410
so that people would have these skills

00:46:49.410 --> 00:46:51.360
being taught within that system,

00:46:51.360 --> 00:46:52.730
and have these skills to approach things.

00:46:52.730 --> 00:46:54.840
So even as you mentioned, these are not,

00:46:54.840 --> 00:46:55.950
oh, you only need these,

00:46:55.950 --> 00:46:58.260
if you're starting to
navigate a mental illness,

00:46:58.260 --> 00:46:59.250
but these are life skills,

00:46:59.250 --> 00:47:01.276
mental wellness that we
should be incorporating.

00:47:01.276 --> 00:47:02.520
So I'm wondering, though,

00:47:02.520 --> 00:47:04.710
in the work that you all have done,

00:47:04.710 --> 00:47:05.910
even the work that your wife has done,

00:47:05.910 --> 00:47:07.440
how has that perspective

00:47:07.440 --> 00:47:09.420
in terms of thinking about education,

00:47:09.420 --> 00:47:11.340
and some of the challenges
around mental health,

00:47:11.340 --> 00:47:12.210
and what needs to happen there,

00:47:12.210 --> 00:47:15.150
how has that perspective
also impacted the work

00:47:15.150 --> 00:47:16.980
that you've been doing?

00:47:16.980 --> 00:47:18.090
- Well, if you think about it,

00:47:18.090 --> 00:47:23.090
you got all these point
source solutions for obesity,

00:47:23.370 --> 00:47:26.640
or sleep, or what have you.

00:47:26.640 --> 00:47:31.380
We basically need a learning
management system for life

00:47:31.380 --> 00:47:34.770
where we kind of like the internet model,

00:47:34.770 --> 00:47:36.840
we understand how the whole thing works.

00:47:36.840 --> 00:47:40.737
We know how to do Google,
and we know how to do,

00:47:40.737 --> 00:47:42.600
but we need to have,

00:47:42.600 --> 00:47:47.577
now some of us may have how do
we overcome these delusions?

00:47:48.720 --> 00:47:52.207
And, frankly, people
with psychotic disorders,

00:47:52.207 --> 00:47:56.703
there is cognitive
behavioral therapy on how to,

00:47:56.703 --> 00:47:59.670
and there's even with suicide ideation.

00:47:59.670 --> 00:48:02.700
It's not just simply a
matter of depression.

00:48:02.700 --> 00:48:04.500
And I know John Krystal and others at Yale

00:48:04.500 --> 00:48:06.087
have been really formative in this.

00:48:06.087 --> 00:48:10.830
It's about a particular
type of impulse control

00:48:10.830 --> 00:48:12.060
that you can actually do

00:48:12.060 --> 00:48:13.530
cognitive behavioral therapy around,

00:48:13.530 --> 00:48:16.650
and actually reduce the
suicide rate in this country.

00:48:16.650 --> 00:48:18.300
And yet we don't use that,

00:48:18.300 --> 00:48:20.520
especially when we have
people come get admitted

00:48:20.520 --> 00:48:22.560
in ERs for suicide ideation,

00:48:22.560 --> 00:48:24.570
we don't immediately say, hey, by the way,

00:48:24.570 --> 00:48:28.028
there's this program where
you can dramatically reduce

00:48:28.028 --> 00:48:31.710
the likelihood of you ever
dying by your own hand,

00:48:31.710 --> 00:48:34.470
because now you will
have the tools necessary

00:48:34.470 --> 00:48:39.360
when you feel that
desperation to not act on it.

00:48:39.360 --> 00:48:44.310
And if you could get the results in cancer

00:48:44.310 --> 00:48:45.900
that you can get in mental health

00:48:45.900 --> 00:48:48.390
by adopting these evidence-based,

00:48:48.390 --> 00:48:49.890
I mean, it'd be a no-brainer.

00:48:49.890 --> 00:48:52.410
Plus, we'd be paid for it, Dr. Addy.

00:48:52.410 --> 00:48:54.807
We have greater therapeutic interventions

00:48:54.807 --> 00:48:59.790
that could make a bigger
survival difference

00:48:59.790 --> 00:49:02.710
than all of these other medical services

00:49:04.903 --> 00:49:09.675
that get reimbursed oodles
of dollars to pay for those,

00:49:09.675 --> 00:49:14.675
and only move the disease
burden on the margins, right?

00:49:15.150 --> 00:49:17.370
Compared to what would
happen in this space.

00:49:17.370 --> 00:49:20.160
So I would say that

00:49:20.160 --> 00:49:23.040
I really believe we need to understand

00:49:23.040 --> 00:49:25.680
kind of at a basic level
what's the handbook in life?

00:49:25.680 --> 00:49:28.560
How do we understand
our brain how it works?

00:49:28.560 --> 00:49:31.889
How do we maximize our own challenges?

00:49:31.889 --> 00:49:32.730
By the way,

00:49:32.730 --> 00:49:35.670
that could apply to personal
relationships for some.

00:49:35.670 --> 00:49:38.580
It could apply to drugs
and alcohol for others.

00:49:38.580 --> 00:49:42.933
It could apply to workplace issues.

00:49:44.060 --> 00:49:48.030
It's the mechanisms that are the same,

00:49:48.030 --> 00:49:49.830
no matter what your choice

00:49:49.830 --> 00:49:52.739
of what's your bedevilments, right?

00:49:52.739 --> 00:49:55.950
And that's why the 12-step program

00:49:55.950 --> 00:49:58.800
generally is worked across gambling,

00:49:58.800 --> 00:50:03.720
and narcotics, and alcoholism,
and obesity, and all,

00:50:03.720 --> 00:50:06.780
because the principles
are what are universal.

00:50:06.780 --> 00:50:09.780
We need to embed that kind of principles

00:50:09.780 --> 00:50:12.180
in a learning management system

00:50:12.180 --> 00:50:17.070
that allows you to customize, if you will,

00:50:17.070 --> 00:50:20.100
those principles for you in your own life.

00:50:20.100 --> 00:50:23.640
So you learn how to break
that default thinking

00:50:23.640 --> 00:50:27.180
as it applies to you, like
everyone's always after me,

00:50:27.180 --> 00:50:28.470
or they're all trying to get me,

00:50:28.470 --> 00:50:29.798
or I'm full of resentment.

00:50:29.798 --> 00:50:32.280
Well, let's think about
how to dismantle that

00:50:32.280 --> 00:50:34.290
because it's taking me offline

00:50:34.290 --> 00:50:36.099
from being able to achieve
what I wanna achieve.

00:50:36.099 --> 00:50:41.099
Americans love looking
better, feeling better.

00:50:42.120 --> 00:50:45.390
Why not make this the thing that we want,

00:50:45.390 --> 00:50:49.680
because if it's happiness
we're all for, right?

00:50:49.680 --> 00:50:52.620
Fulfillment, contentment,

00:50:52.620 --> 00:50:57.480
the best way to sell that
is to make that sort of

00:50:57.480 --> 00:51:00.720
uniquely American consumerism apply to

00:51:00.720 --> 00:51:04.173
how do I become a better consumer
for my own mental health?

00:51:05.820 --> 00:51:08.250
So that's how I would think about it.

00:51:08.250 --> 00:51:10.860
Of course, in education,

00:51:10.860 --> 00:51:14.160
you've got that universal
public health approach.

00:51:14.160 --> 00:51:17.340
So you're not trying to
play catch up down the line

00:51:17.340 --> 00:51:21.900
when I'm in my 20s trying to
get recovered from addiction.

00:51:21.900 --> 00:51:26.900
If I had earlier on been
equipped to be more aware,

00:51:29.910 --> 00:51:33.360
I wouldn't let myself
constantly be kind of

00:51:33.360 --> 00:51:36.420
deluded into thinking that
I still have got this.

00:51:36.420 --> 00:51:38.583
Like, I could still manage my life,

00:51:39.480 --> 00:51:42.990
which is what is the great unifying factor

00:51:42.990 --> 00:51:44.243
of all people with
addiction, and mental illness

00:51:44.243 --> 00:51:47.160
is that they think they're
not as bad as they really are.

00:51:47.160 --> 00:51:50.836
It's that denial that we talk about,

00:51:50.836 --> 00:51:54.750
but education's where we
could lay the foundation

00:51:54.750 --> 00:51:57.240
for a really strong
future on mental health.

00:51:57.240 --> 00:51:58.830
